{"results": [{"id": "background-supplement.json", "pointer": "", "view": "value", "value": {"policy": "mathematical-background-v2-1", "graph_sha256": "b428677b6ba6196d92e79d257ce09852946cf432c962d7b9bc0674adaec47259", "spec_sha256": "7297c1424bfba078c08dfb8137cfb667f100847b54dcaa0e54ecdd3bab44dd3f", "notes": {"R001": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "For s>0 and finite δ>0, the infimum of the nonnegative covering costs in M001 on ℝ^m at scale δ equals H^s_δ(A) in D002. Countable covers and ℕ-indexed covers give the same costs, and their real versus extended diameter conventions agree at this scale.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/0/definition", "reason": "On a bounded Euclidean set its extended diameter is the embedded finite real diameter; all admissible members have diameter at most δ."}, {"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "D002 uses countable covers, diameter bound ≤δ, positive exponent, and zero cost for empty members."}, {"location": "/inputs/0/statement", "reason": "M001 uses sequences, the same diameter bound, and zero cost for empty members."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "The argument fixes Euclidean A, assumes s>0, and compares an arbitrary finite positive scale."}], "justification": "Embed the finite nonnegative diameters into [0,∞]; positive real powers of finite values agree with their extended counterparts, including 0^s=0. Thus corresponding cover members have equal costs. Enumerate a countably infinite cover bijectively by ℕ; pad a finite or empty family with empty members. Conversely a sequence is a countable indexed family. Reindexing a nonnegative sum preserves it, since the sum is the supremum of finite subsums, and inserting zero terms preserves those suprema. The admissible cost sets therefore have equal infima."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "Let C(r) be the M001 infimum of cover costs at scale r∈(0,∞]. For 0<r≤t≤∞, C(r)≥C(t). Consequently sup_{r∈(0,∞]} C(r)=sup_{δ∈ℝ,δ>0} C(δ), with finite scales embedded in [0,∞].", "grounds": [{"location": "/inputs/0/statement", "reason": "The only dependence of admissibility on r is diam(T_k)≤r; all infima and suprema take values in [0,∞]."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "The proof compares the extended-scale supremum with all finite positive real scales."}], "justification": "Every r-admissible cover is t-admissible for r≤t, so the infimum over the smaller collection is at least that over the larger. Every positive finite extended scale is the embedding of a positive real. The supremum over all scales is at least the finite-scale supremum. In the reverse direction all finite-scale terms are bounded by that supremum, and C(∞)≤C(1), which is also bounded by it. These order comparisons prove equality without interchanging limits or assuming an infimum is attained."}], "R002": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "A compact subset K of Euclidean ℝ^n is bounded and is contained in [-L,L]^n for some finite real L≥1, also for n=0.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inputs/1/statement", "reason": "H002 supplies compactness of E."}, {"location": "/parameters", "reason": "E lies in finite-dimensional Euclidean ℝ^n."}, {"location": "/definitions/0/definition", "reason": "The Euclidean metric and the singleton convention in dimension zero are specified."}], "justification": "The open balls about the Euclidean origin of positive integer radius co…2007 tokens truncated…ibility."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/2/text", "reason": "0<s<1 and 0<ρ_i≤δ give r=s−1<0; zero diameters are treated using s>0."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/3/text", "reason": "For fixed 0<s<1, p=1−s>0 and δ ranges over every positive scale."}], "justification": "The exponential representation gives the exponent identity and reverses monotonicity when the exponent is negative. The logarithm tends to infinity at infinity and to minus infinity at zero, while exp tends to infinity and to zero at the corresponding ends. Applying these facts with p>0 proves the two power limits. Thus vanishing costs apply at each fixed positive scale, and the lower bounds δ^(s−1) exceed every finite number at sufficiently small scales."}], "R004": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "For q>0,r>0, H^q(E)=0 implies H^q_r(E)=0. If this scale content is zero, then for every η>0 there is an admissible countable cover with total q-cost <η; such a cover can be indexed by ℕ by inserting empty members without changing its cost or coverage.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "Scale content is the infimum of nonnegative countable-cover costs; measure is the supremum over positive scales, and empty sets cost zero."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "N001 and N002 with L2–L4 give q>0, r>0, H^q(E)=0 and η=ε/2>0."}], "justification": "Each nonnegative scale content is bounded above by the zero supremum. If all admissible costs were at least η, their infimum would be at least η, contradicting zero; an empty admissible class has infimum ∞ and is excluded too. A countable family can be enumerated or placed at distinct natural indices; fill unused indices with empty sets. Reindexing nonnegative sums and adding zero terms preserves the cost."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "A nonempty subset of a metric space with diameter zero is a singleton. For n≥1, x∈ℝ^n and d>0, the open ball B(x,d/2) contains x and has diameter exactly d.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/0/definition", "reason": "The diameter is the supremum of pairwise distances in the supplied Euclidean metric."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "L1 gives n≥1."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "K consists of the nonempty W_i; each d_i is positive and finite, as justified by BG003."}], "justification": "Every pairwise distance in a zero-diameter set is zero, so metric separation identifies all its points with any selected member. In the ball, the triangle inequality bounds all pairwise distances by d. A first-coordinate unit vector e exists because n≥1. For 0<t<d/2 the points x±te lie in the ball and have distance 2t. These distances approach d as t increases to d/2, giving the reverse supremum bound; equality is of the supremum and does not require an attained pair."}, {"id": "BG003", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "For q,r,ε>0 and i∈ℕ, d_i=min(r,(ε·2^(-i-3))^(1/q)) is positive, is at most r, and satisfies d_i^q≤ε·2^(-i-3).", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "L2, N002, L3 and L4 establish q,r,ε>0."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "This is the definition of d_i for i∈K⊆ℕ."}], "justification": "Both entries of the minimum are positive finite reals. On positive reals the map t↦t^q is increasing for q>0 and ((a)^(1/q))^q=a. These power identities follow from t^u=exp(u log t), the inverse identities for exp and log, and their strict monotonicity. Apply this with a=ε·2^(-i-3)>0 and d_i≤a^(1/q)."}, {"id": "BG004", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "For ε>0, sum_{i=0}^∞ ε·2^(-i-3)=ε/4. If original nonnegative costs a_i have sum A<ε/2, and modified costs on K⊆ℕ satisfy b_i≤a_i+ε·2^(-i-3), then sum_{i∈K} b_i≤A+ε/4<ε.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement", "reason": "F001 supplies a_i=diam(W_i)^q≥0 with A<ε/2."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "Unchanged members have b_i=a_i, singleton originals have a_i=0 and replacement b_i=d_i^q≤ε·2^(-i-3); K⊆ℕ."}], "justification": "The partial sum through M is ε/4·(1-2^(-(M+1))), tending to ε/4 since 2^(-(M+1))→0. For any finite J⊆K, summing the pointwise bound gives sum_J b_i≤sum_J a_i+sum_J ε·2^(-i-3)≤A+ε/4. Taking the supremum over finite J, the definition of the nonnegative series, gives the claimed infinite-sum bound without any exchange of limits. Finally A+ε/4<3ε/4<ε."}], "R005": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "If f:A→B is a bijective isometry between Euclidean subsets, then for every s>0 and δ>0 their ambient Hausdorff contents H^s_δ agree, hence H^s(A)=H^s(B).", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "N002 at n=0 identifies E with ℝ^0, and the displayed coordinate map is a bijection I→F."}, {"location": "/definitions/5/definition", "reason": "The Euclidean product distance makes this coordinate map an isometry."}, {"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "Contents are infima over countable ambient covers, with positive-exponent empty cost zero."}], "justification": "Intersect any cover of A with A and map the intersections by f to obtain a countable cover of B with no greater diameters; empty intersections cause no cost. Apply the inverse isometry to intersected covers of B for the reverse inequality. Infima and then suprema over the same positive scales give equality."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "fact": "For 0<ρ≤1, N=ceil(1/ρ) is a positive finite integer with 1/N≤ρ and N≤2/ρ. The N closed intervals [j/N,(j+1)/N], 0≤j<N, cover [0,1]. If diam(U)≤ρ in ℝ^n and J is one such interval, then diam(U×J)≤√2ρ in the specified Euclidean metric.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement", "reason": "F002 gives 0<diam(U_i)≤r."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "r=min(1,δ/√2)≤1."}, {"location": "/definitions/5/definition", "reason": "Squared product distance is the sum of squared coordinate distances."}], "justification": "The integer ceiling obeys x≤ceil(x)≤x+1; apply x=1/ρ≥1 and invert positive quantities for 1/N≤ρ. The adjacent subdivision intervals cover [0,1] and have diameter 1/N. For any two product points each coordinate distance is≤ρ, so their squared Euclidean distance is≤2ρ². Taking nonnegative square roots and then the diameter supremum proves the bound."}, {"id": "BG003", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "fact": "For K⊆ℕ and positive finite integers N_i, the set of pairs (i,j) with i∈K and 0≤j<N_i is at most countable. For nonnegative costs c_ij, its total cost equals ∑_{i∈K}∑_{j<N_i}c_ij. Termwise inequalities and multiplication by finite nonnegative constants preserve these sums.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/1/produces/0/statement", "reason": "K⊆ℕ and the covering family are supplied by F002."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/2/text", "reason": "Each N_i is a positive finite integer and each product cost is nonnegative; ∑r_i^q<ε."}], "justification": "The pair index set injects into ℕ×ℕ, which admits a diagonal enumeration. A nonnegative countable sum is the supremum of its finite subsums. Every finite pair set meets finitely many rows, and finite row subsums are themselves finite pair subsums; these two comparisons give the grouping equality. The same definition gives termwise monotonicity and finite scalar multiplication, including when an intermediate sum is infinite."}, {"id": "BG004", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "fact": "For s>0, real powers on nonnegative finite bases are increasing, with 0^s=0. On positive bases they satisfy (ab)^s=a^s b^s and ρ^(q+1)/ρ=ρ^q. Consequently, for q>0, 0<ρ≤1, N≤2/ρ, and d_j≤√2ρ, one has ∑_{j<N}d_j^(q+1)≤2(√2)^(q+1)ρ^q.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "N002 yields q>0."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/2/text", "reason": "The displayed construction gives positive r_i≤1, N_i≤2/r_i and product diameters≤√2r_i."}, {"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "The cover cost uses the positive exponent q+1."}], "justification": "For positive bases, a^s=exp(s log a); monotonicity of log and exp and their addition identities give the asserted power laws and monotonicity. The zero base follows from the positive-exponent convention. Bound each of the N costs by (√2ρ)^(q+1), multiply by N≤2/ρ, and use the positive-base identities to obtain the displayed estimate. The constant 2(√2)^(q+1) is finite and positive for finite real q."}, {"id": "BG005", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "fact": "For a finite C>0 and x∈[0,∞], if x<Cε for every ε>0 then x=0. A supremum of zero scale contents is zero. For finite real D and b∈[0,∞], if b≤q+1 for every real q>D then b≤D+1; likewise b≤a for all a>1 implies b≤1.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/2/produces/0/statement", "reason": "F003 holds for each ε>0 at fixed q>D and δ>0."}, {"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "Contents are nonnegative infima and H is their supremum over δ>0."}, {"location": "/inputs/1/statement", "reason": "D is finite and nonnegative."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/3/text", "reason": "N001 supplies b=dim_H(F)≤q+1 at every q>D."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "The n=0 branch supplies b≤a for every a>1."}], "justification": "Choosing ε=1 first rules out x=∞. If finite x>0, choosing ε=x/(2C)>0 contradicts x<Cε; hence x=0. Supremum of a family all equal to zero is zero. Choosing q=D+1 first makes b finite; if b>D+1, choose q=D+(b-D-1)/2, so q>D and q+1<b, a contradiction. For the last assertion a=2 first makes b finite, and if b>1 choose a=(1+b)/2. These are order arguments and involve no interchange of limits with sums or infima."}], "R006": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "If B⊆ℝ is nonempty and bounded, then a=inf B and b=sup B are finite, a≤x≤b for x∈B, and diam(B)=b−a. For η>0, B⊆(a−η/2,b+η/2), whose Lebesgue length is b−a+η.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "F001 establishes nonempty bounded B_i with diam(B_i)≤ρ_i<∞."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "ε>0 and 1+ρ_i^s>0 give finite η_i>0."}], "justification": "Completeness gives finite extrema bounds. Every pairwise distance is ≤b−a; points of B arbitrarily near each extremal bound give the reverse supremum inequality (also when a=b). Positive η/2 makes both endpoint inequalities strict. Lebesgue length of the resulting finite open interval is its endpoint difference."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "For K⊆ℕ, ε>0, and finite a_i≥0, the nonnegative series Σ_{i∈K} ε·2^(−i−1)·a_i/(1+a_i) is at most ε.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "K⊆ℕ and ρ_i are finite nonnegative reals."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "ε>0 and a_i=ρ_i^s≥0 for s>0; these are exactly η_iρ_i^s."}], "justification": "The ratio a_i/(1+a_i) lies in [0,1]. For every finite subset of K its sum is bounded by ε times an initial partial sum of 2^(−i−1). The partial sums through i=m−1 equal 1−2^(−m)≤1 and tend to 1. Taking the supremum over finite subsets gives the stated bound."}, {"id": "BG003", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "fact": "For μ=λ|[0,1], finite constants a,c≥0, and a Borel set J⊆ℝ, the function a·1_J is Borel measurable, ∫ a·1_J dμ=a·λ(J∩[0,1]), and ∫ c dμ=c. Nonnegative integrals are monotone, and λ(J∩[0,1])≤λ(J). Thus a pointwise bound c≤Σ_i g_i on [0,1] can be integrated against μ.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/4", "reason": "I is the Borel interval [0,1]."}, {"location": "/definitions/7", "reason": "λ and restriction/integral notation are supplied."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "c and each weight ρ_i^s are finite nonnegative."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "Every J_i is a bounded open interval; g_i=ρ_i^s1_{J_i} is Borel measurable."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/2/text", "reason": "F003 supplies the pointwise inequality on I."}], "justification": "Indicators of Borel sets are measurable, and multiplication by a finite constant preserves measurability. The simple-function definition of the nonnegative integral gives ∫a1_J=aμ(J). Restriction gives μ(J)=λ(J∩I); λ(I)=1 by the endpoint length formula. Monotonicity follows from the supremum over dominated simple functions, and measure monotonicity gives the intersection bound. The restriction assigns zero measure to I's complement, so an inequality on I is enough. These basic integral properties do not supply the interchange of sum and integral, which is exactly the explicit input M006."}, {"id": "BG004", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "fact": "For nonnegative countable families u_i,v_i, Σ_i(u_i+v_i)=Σ_i u_i+Σ_i v_i in [0,∞], and termwise inequalities pass to sums. For finite ρ≥0 and s>0, ρ^s·ρ=ρ^(s+1), including ρ=0.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "K is countable, ρ_i is finite nonnegative, and s>0."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "η_i>0 is finite, so all terms in S004 are nonnegative."}, {"location": "/definitions/7", "reason": "Nonnegative sums mean suprema of finite partial sums."}], "justification": "Finite distributivity gives the sum identity on finite subsets. Taking suprema gives ≤; for the reverse inequality combine finite subsets approximating the two sums into their finite union. This also handles infinite sums. Termwise comparison follows on each finite subset. For positive ρ the power identity follows from exp((s+1)logρ)=exp(slogρ)·exp(logρ); at zero both sides vanish because s and s+1 are positive."}, {"id": "BG005", "kind": "elementary", "at": "/inference/argument/4/text", "fact": "For finite c≥0 and T∈[0,∞], if c≤T+ε for every real ε>0, then c≤T.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "N002 gives finite c."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/3/text", "reason": "F004 holds for arbitrary ε>0 with T=Σ_iρ_i^(s+1), independent of ε."}], "justification": "If T=∞ the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise, if c>T, choose ε=(c−T)/2>0; then T+ε<c contradicts the premise. This removes ε by finite order reasoning and requires no interchange of limits."}], "R007": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "For a nonempty family (a_t) in [0,∞], sup_t a_t=∞ if and only if for every finite real M>0 some a_t>M. Also, Q∈[0,∞] exceeds every finite M>0 if and only if Q=∞. In particular these facts apply to the contents indexed by positive scales in S001 and S002.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/1/definition", "reason": "H^a(A) is the supremum of H^a_δ(A) over all positive real δ, a nonempty index set; its values are in [0,∞]."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "N001 supplies H^s(E)=∞ under 0<s<D."}], "justification": "If no family member exceeds M, M is a finite upper bound and the supremum cannot be ∞. Conversely, a finite supremum would bound all members and contradict the property for M larger than that supremum. Likewise, if Q were finite, choosing M=Q+1 contradicts Q>M. The defining supremum is at least each scale value, which validates the strict threshold transfer in S002."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "fact": "If 0<d<∞ is real and q∈[0,∞] satisfies q≥s+1 for every real s with 0<s<d, then q≥d+1.", "grounds": [{"location": "/inputs/1/statement", "reason": "N002 gives 0≤D≤n<∞."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "L1 gives D>0, and s is arbitrary under L2: 0<s<D."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/2/text", "reason": "N001 and F002 give dim_H(F)≥s+1 throughout that interval."}], "justification": "The case q=∞ is immediate. If finite q<d+1, then a=max(0,q−1)<d. The real number s=(a+d)/2 satisfies 0<s<d and s>q−1, so s+1>q, contradicting the assumed inequality. Thus d+1 is a lower bound for q. This verifies the displayed supremum passage without requiring a measure-continuity theorem or an interchange of limits."}], "R008": [{"id": "BG001", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "fact": "For the isometry f:I→F established here, every s>0 and δ>0 satisfy H^s_δ(I)≤H^s_δ(F), and consequently H^s(I)≤H^s(F).", "grounds": [{"location": "/inference/argument/0/text", "reason": "The chosen x0∈E makes f(t)=(x0,t) a map into F, with dist(f(t),f(u))=|t-u|."}, {"location": "/definitions/0", "reason": "Diameter is the supremum of pairwise distances, with empty diameter zero."}, {"location": "/definitions/1", "reason": "Contents are infima of countable covering costs and measures are suprema over positive scales, at positive exponents."}], "justification": "For a countable δ-cover (U_i) of F put V_i={t∈I:f(t)∈U_i}. These sets cover I. Every pair in V_i maps to a pair in U_i at equal distance, so diam(V_i)≤diam(U_i), also when V_i is empty. Since s>0, the function r↦r^s is increasing on nonnegative reals; all these diameters are at most the finite δ. Thus each pulled-back cost is no greater, and the same holds for countable sums, defined by suprema of finite partial sums. H^s_δ(I) is therefore a lower bound for all costs of δ-covers of F, proving its comparison with their infimum. Taking suprema over the same positive scales preserves the inequality. This expands the packet's displayed cover argument."}, {"id": "BG002", "kind": "mathematical", "at": "/inference/argument/1/text", "fact": "If q∈[0,∞] satisfies q≥s for every real s with 0<s<1, then q≥1.", "grounds": [{"location": "/definitions/3", "reason": "dim_H(F) takes values in [0,∞]."}, {"location": "/inference/argument/1/text", "reason": "N001 applied to F001 gives dim_H(F)≥s for every 0<s<1."}], "justification": "If q<1 then q is a finite nonnegative real r<1. The real s=(r+1)/2 satisfies 0<s<1 and r<s, contradicting q≥s. Thus q≥1. This is the order content of 'let s↑1'; it requires no convergence theorem or finite